Subject: Tentative language in the PEPS USer Guide with regard to the 
problematic third-party PDF creaters.
To: pepswin <Unknown>


This is a draft copy of the verbiage going into the Buide.  All references 
to appendicies are fantasies at this point because I am just starting to 
write some of them.  However, the rest of the document is starting to look 
like a "useful something".

Since this is such a sore-point issue, I am giving the entire group 
membership a chance to critique the documentation on this part.  Note:  
Appendix Z or whatever it becomes will be 100% technical specifics on how 
to find the files after you obtain a download and install them [and later 
optionally uninstall them].  Copy the relevant files to within PEPS and in 
one case, you have to edit one of the textual .ini files from the author 
because it needs some tweaking for use here, etc.  But the package 
distribution will not actually have the files themselves.  Also note: One 
of these files has a watermark problem unless you purchase the shareware; I 
cannot police how the vendor sells his product, but I can offer this:

1)  This is a $100 package which is pretty steep for such a buggy product.  
It apparently hasn't been worked on since around 2005 and the price is 
perhaps way too high today for such a n arguably decrepit package neglected 
for more than a decade [but it does work well enough for our purposes].  
Also, as provided, it is arguably not the greatest way to use this class of 
product, and certainly isn't the way competing analogous packages are 
presented.

2) We are only caring about a small fraction of a larger package, and 
worse, it is very buggy.  It cannot work with any other font than the one I 
am using.  It ALMOST does a few others, but it is hopelessly buggy on the 
others, and arguably even if they worked, they are pretty ugly.  The 
overall method is a very obsolete and ancient method that comes out of the 
days of Adobe Postscript defining fonts for printers, etc.  The freeware 
product uses conforming Windows fonts which is a good step forward, but it 
lacks the basic frill as to why we care about the shareware one at all.  I 
may drop subtle hints suggesting you can end-run the need to register the 
product to get around the watermark problem, but the official story is that 
page one of any output has the unregistered co[y watermark.  [Note: This 
isn't much of a lock in the days of editable PDF documents using excellent 
freeware that you can just download; the only connection that might make 
sense to PEPS would be to include a convenient startup icon for the package 
in the PEPS command window for minor convenience.  But, I have no 
connection with either of these authors, and this way, they have to deal 
with you, the users, and not me.  You don't want me to be sued over a 
public domain 12-bit computing project that will never raise dime one, do 
you!  [But authors sometimes have lawyers who can talk people into 
lawsuits, etc.

Anyway, what follows is the text ripped out of my mighty word-processor 
JARTE.  So, don't complain about formatting, just the words!

cjl

__________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

d) Convert LPT to PDF.  The contents of the LPTOUT.TXT (LPT:)
        file is converted to the file LPTOUT.PDF in the
       SIMH_Working_Files directory using the text2pdf freeware
       program from VeryPDF.

       The output is presented in 11" x 17" wide lineprinter paper
       format with a white background.  Any conforming Windows font
       may be used; the default distribution font is Consolas 10.
       Font particulars can be changed by modification of the
       TXT2PDF.BAT program located in the SIMH_Utilities directory.

       Note: Not all Windows-compatible fonts are fully compatible
       with the VeryPDF program; advanced users may consider
       carefully attempting to use alternate fonts.

       The key VeryPDF utility program file is not included in the
       standard release of the PEPS package for legal reasons.  PEPS
       users may obtain the file from the author[s] of VeryPDF.
       Appendix Z provides furher information on obtaining this file
       and how to add it to a working copy of the PEPS package.

       Most PDF file readers can adjust the display size for easy
       viewing and output to printers of varying sizes.  It is
       strongly recommended that Adobe Reader Version 11.x be
       installed on the host Windows system.  Appendix F contains
       more information regarding recommended Windows add-on
       utilities and recommended settings/patches.

    e) Convert LPT to PDF [Greenbar].  The contents of the LPTOUT.TXT 
       (LPT:) file is converted to the file LPTOUT.PDF in the
       SIMH_Working_Files directory using the text2pdf shareware
       program from DaVince Tools.

       The output is presented in 11" x 8.5" landscape compressed
       lineprinter paper format with a greenbar background.  Output
       font is permanently set to Courier Bold 9.

       Key DaVince Tools utility program files are not included in
       the standard release of the PEPS package for legal reasons.
       PEPS users may obtain the files from the author[s] of DaVince
       Tools.  Appendix Z provides further information on obtaining
       these files and how to add them to a working copy of the PEPS
       package.

       Most PDF file readers can adjust the display size and
       orientation for easy viewing and output to printers of varying
       sizes.  It is strongly recommended that Adobe Reader Version
       11.x be installed on the host Windows system.  Appendix F
       contains more information regarding recommended Windows add-on
       utilities and recommended settings/patches.


